# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

Enables the provisioning and management of Redpanda clusters on AWS and GCP. A cluster must always have a network and resource group.

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Attributes that force cluster replacement

Some attributes cannot be updated in place. Changing any of them causes Terraform to plan a replacement: the existing cluster is destroyed and a new one is created, resulting in data loss.

The `allow_deletion` attribute gates this behavior. With `allow_deletion = false` (the default), an apply that requires replacement fails with `cluster deletion not allowed` and the cluster is preserved. With `allow_deletion = true`, Terraform destroys and recreates the cluster, so always review the plan output for attributes marked `forces replacement` before applying.

Changing any of the following top-level attributes forces replacement:

- `cloud_provider`
- `cluster_type`
- `connection_type`
- `network_id`
- `redpanda_version`
- `region`
- `resource_group_id`
- `zones`

For BYOVPC clusters, the nested `customer_managed_resources` attributes (AWS resource ARNs, GCP service account emails, and GCP subnet names) also force replacement.

Note: in provider versions 1.4.1 and earlier, changing `throughput_tier` also forced replacement. Since version 1.4.2, throughput tier changes are applied in place.

